1
00:01:21,715 --> 00:01:23,513
Defendant will rise.

2
00:01:25,786 --> 00:01:28,517
Will the foreperson
please read the verdict?

3
00:01:29,423 --> 00:01:32,188
It is the recommendation
of this jury

4
00:01:32,359 --> 00:01:35,261
that in view of the
aggravated circumstances

5
00:01:35,429 --> 00:01:37,864
under which this
murder was committed,

6
00:01:38,031 --> 00:01:40,933
the defendant, Adam Spangler,

7
00:01:41,101 --> 00:01:44,333
be given punishment by death.

8
00:01:47,174 --> 00:01:49,006
Request that the jury be polled.

9
00:01:49,176 --> 00:01:51,543
Juror number one, is this
your recommendation?

10
00:01:51,712 --> 00:01:53,472
- Yes, it is.
- Juror number two?

11
00:01:54,181 --> 00:01:55,911
- Yes, it is. JUDGE:
Juror number three?

12
00:01:56,383 --> 00:01:57,715
It is, Your Honor.

13
00:01:57,884 --> 00:01:59,250
Juror number four?

14
00:01:59,419 --> 00:02:00,419
Yes, Your Honor.

15
00:02:00,587 --> 00:02:01,680
Juror number five?

16
00:02:01,855 --> 00:02:03,187
It is, Your Honor.

17
00:02:03,357 --> 00:02:04,689
Juror number six?

18
00:02:04,858 --> 00:02:05,985
Yes, Your Honor.

19
00:02:06,159 --> 00:02:07,388
Juror number seven?

20
00:02:07,561 --> 00:02:08,824
Yes, Your Honor.

21
00:02:08,996 --> 00:02:10,396
Juror number eight?

22
00:02:10,564 --> 00:02:11,930
Yes, it is, Your Honor.

23
00:02:12,099 --> 00:02:13,260
Juror number nine?

24
00:02:13,433 --> 00:02:14,958
It is, Your Honor.

25
00:02:15,135 --> 00:02:16,262
Juror number 10?

26
00:02:16,436 --> 00:02:17,836
Yes, it is.

27
00:02:18,005 --> 00:02:19,496
Juror number 11?

28
00:02:19,673 --> 00:02:21,266
It is, Your Honor.

29
00:02:21,441 --> 00:02:22,909
Juror number 12?

30
00:02:23,076 --> 00:02:24,669
Yes.

31
00:02:24,845 --> 00:02:27,337
Thank you, ladies and
gentlemen of the jury.

32
00:02:27,514 --> 00:02:31,212
Mr. Spangler, based upon
the jury recommendations

33
00:02:31,385 --> 00:02:34,287
and my review of
the probation reports,

34
00:02:35,322 --> 00:02:40,022
it is the sentence of this
court that you will be executed

35
00:02:40,327 --> 00:02:41,852
under the laws of
the state of Georgia.

36
00:02:42,029 --> 00:02:45,989
Said execution to take place
at a time to be determined

37
00:02:46,166 --> 00:02:49,364
by the Commissioner of the
Department of Corrections.

38
00:02:52,105 --> 00:02:53,698
May God have mercy on your soul.

39
00:03:01,181 --> 00:03:03,309
How could you let this happen?

40
00:03:04,151 --> 00:03:06,882
The DA rested his case on
the testimony of one witness,

41
00:03:07,054 --> 00:03:09,114
on what some crotchety
old lady thinks she saw,

42
00:03:09,289 --> 00:03:10,814
and you couldn't get me off?

43
00:03:10,991 --> 00:03:12,926
You have no one to
blame but yourself.

44
00:03:13,093 --> 00:03:16,120
Ha. You're right. For hiring
an incompetent jerk like you.

45
00:03:16,296 --> 00:03:19,357
It all boiled down to
her word against yours.

46
00:03:19,700 --> 00:03:20,793
When you got on the stand,

47
00:03:20,967 --> 00:03:22,727
when your one chance
to make them believe you

48
00:03:22,869 --> 00:03:25,065
instead of her rolled
around, you blew it.

49
00:03:25,906 --> 00:03:27,450
You smiled, you laughed,
you were charming...

50
00:03:27,474 --> 00:03:28,772
I'm innocent, damn it.

51
00:03:28,942 --> 00:03:30,376
I didn't do it.

52
00:03:30,544 --> 00:03:32,843
The jury didn't
believe you, Adam.

53
00:03:33,814 --> 00:03:36,340
They saw you for the
two-bit con man you are.

54
00:03:36,516 --> 00:03:37,984
I should never
have listened to you.

55
00:03:38,151 --> 00:03:39,744
I did the best I could.

56
00:03:39,920 --> 00:03:43,448
Oh, yeah? Then how come I
just got sentenced to death?

57
00:03:44,658 --> 00:03:46,422
I didn't kill that woman.

58
00:03:46,593 --> 00:03:48,186
I'm innocent.

59
00:03:49,596 --> 00:03:52,691
Remember that while you're
teeing off this afternoon.

60
00:03:52,866 --> 00:03:54,459
I'm very sorry.

61
00:03:58,872 --> 00:04:00,500
Adam.

62
00:04:26,800 --> 00:04:28,826
Here. Here.

63
00:04:29,002 --> 00:04:31,836
"Press enter."

64
00:04:34,174 --> 00:04:35,198
Mm.

65
00:04:37,811 --> 00:04:40,337
Oh, come on.

66
00:04:41,782 --> 00:04:42,841
Okay.

67
00:04:47,087 --> 00:04:49,386
Now, cut that out.

68
00:04:52,926 --> 00:04:56,590
Okay. Okay. Hm...

69
00:04:59,032 --> 00:05:00,591
I said cut that out.

70
00:05:16,650 --> 00:05:19,779
Well, Detective Bouchard, what
a surprise. Come in. Come in.

71
00:05:19,953 --> 00:05:21,798
Sure it's okay? If you're
busy, I can come back.

72
00:05:21,822 --> 00:05:24,519
Oh, no, no. I got myself one
of those personal computers.

73
00:05:24,691 --> 00:05:26,887
Fella I know gave
me a good deal on it.

74
00:05:28,161 --> 00:05:31,359
- You're setting it up yourself?
- Yeah. He said it'd be a piece of cake.

75
00:05:31,531 --> 00:05:33,363
Tough piece of cake.

76
00:05:33,867 --> 00:05:35,699
See you're not
missing any meals.

77
00:05:35,869 --> 00:05:38,532
Not meals, Ben. Just sleep.

78
00:05:38,705 --> 00:05:40,367
Guilty conscience?

79
00:05:40,540 --> 00:05:41,872
In a way.

80
00:05:42,375 --> 00:05:46,005
They sentenced Adam Spangler
today. Got the death penalty.

81
00:05:46,179 --> 00:05:48,546
I never knew anything like
that to bother you before.

82
00:05:48,715 --> 00:05:51,184
No, don't get me wrong.
I'm all for the death penalty.

83
00:05:51,351 --> 00:05:54,617
Hey, I think murderers should die the
same way they made their victims die.

84
00:05:54,788 --> 00:05:59,226
As far as I'm concerned, that would
be justice. And a hell of a deterrent.

85
00:05:59,392 --> 00:06:02,260
Then, why...? I mean, what's...?

86
00:06:03,063 --> 00:06:05,191
I'm not sure he did it.

87
00:06:05,899 --> 00:06:07,800
Well, they had an eyewitness.

88
00:06:07,968 --> 00:06:09,648
Picked him out of a
line-up and everything.

89
00:06:09,803 --> 00:06:11,431
A 70-year-old lady.

90
00:06:11,605 --> 00:06:14,973
Said she saw Adam Spangler enter
the victim's house, burglarize the place,

91
00:06:15,141 --> 00:06:18,168
then shoot the victim in cold
blood when she walked in on him.

92
00:06:18,345 --> 00:06:21,315
But the witness saw all this through
a window in the house next door,

93
00:06:21,481 --> 00:06:23,245
and, Ben, you of all people

94
00:06:23,416 --> 00:06:25,817
know how unreliable that
kind of testimony can be.

95
00:06:25,986 --> 00:06:28,080
Well, jury's convinced.

96
00:06:28,655 --> 00:06:30,180
Just listen to this.

97
00:06:30,357 --> 00:06:33,122
Adam Spangler was seen entering
through the front door of the house,

98
00:06:33,293 --> 00:06:35,228
and a window was broken
in the rear of the house,

99
00:06:35,395 --> 00:06:36,939
so everybody figures
that's the way he went out.

100
00:06:36,963 --> 00:06:39,330
But Spangler did not know
anybody was watching him.

101
00:06:39,833 --> 00:06:42,166
So why didn't he just go
right back out the front?

102
00:06:42,335 --> 00:06:45,396
And we didn't find any of his
fingerprints there. None at all.

103
00:06:45,572 --> 00:06:48,940
Even though the witness
said he wasn't wearing gloves.

104
00:06:49,109 --> 00:06:54,173
And we did lift a set of prints that
didn't match the victim, her relatives,

105
00:06:54,347 --> 00:06:56,509
her housekeeper,
her friends, anybody.

106
00:06:59,386 --> 00:07:00,820
What about the murder weapon?

107
00:07:00,987 --> 00:07:04,321
A 9 mm Makarov, never recovered.

108
00:07:04,491 --> 00:07:05,618
Huh.

109
00:07:05,792 --> 00:07:07,658
What's his story?

110
00:07:07,827 --> 00:07:10,092
- Says he was never there.
- Huh.

111
00:07:10,263 --> 00:07:11,492
No alibi?

112
00:07:11,665 --> 00:07:13,224
- No.
- No.

113
00:07:13,400 --> 00:07:16,564
- Heh-heh-heh.
- Ah, I just got this feeling in my gut.

114
00:07:19,005 --> 00:07:20,268
Your gut, huh?

115
00:07:26,513 --> 00:07:27,674
So you'll talk to him?

116
00:07:36,589 --> 00:07:39,753
Why are you guys always
getting me into this kind of stuff?

117
00:07:40,327 --> 00:07:41,761
Heh-heh. Thanks, Ben.

118
00:07:41,928 --> 00:07:46,491
Oh, uh, now, remember, I'm not
saying this guy's a saint or anything.

119
00:07:46,666 --> 00:07:47,929
He's just no murderer.

120
00:07:48,368 --> 00:07:49,768
I'm gonna hate him, ain't I?

121
00:07:50,437 --> 00:07:53,601
No. He's gonna be perfectly
charming, and you're gonna love him.

122
00:07:53,773 --> 00:07:58,006
It's when you realize that 99 percent
of what he says is absolute bull,

123
00:07:58,178 --> 00:07:59,942
that's when you're
gonna hate him.

124
00:08:01,114 --> 00:08:02,138
Mm.

125
00:08:02,315 --> 00:08:04,079
Transcript of your trial.

126
00:08:04,250 --> 00:08:05,843
I read it last night.

127
00:08:06,019 --> 00:08:08,818
I can certainly see
how you got convicted.

128
00:08:08,989 --> 00:08:11,458
You can't open your
mouth without trying to scam

129
00:08:11,624 --> 00:08:13,217
whoever you're talking to.

130
00:08:14,527 --> 00:08:16,758
- What do you mean?
- You're a grifter,

131
00:08:16,930 --> 00:08:19,092
right down to the
soles of your shoes.

132
00:08:19,265 --> 00:08:20,927
But you're no good at it.

133
00:08:21,101 --> 00:08:24,902
Everything that comes out of your
mouth sounds like a lie. Juries...

134
00:08:28,108 --> 00:08:29,633
It's Campbell Bouchard.

135
00:08:29,809 --> 00:08:31,449
He poisoned your mind
about me, didn't he?

136
00:08:31,578 --> 00:08:34,047
I wouldn't be too
hard on old Campbell.

137
00:08:34,681 --> 00:08:37,879
He's about the only
friend you got right now.

138
00:08:39,085 --> 00:08:42,214
Well, here's the way I see it.

139
00:08:42,889 --> 00:08:47,486
You got a fair trial with
no procedural errors,

140
00:08:47,660 --> 00:08:52,997
and that means that whoever handles
your appeals is gonna be denied.

141
00:08:54,100 --> 00:08:59,038
And that means the only
hope you have is for a new trial.

142
00:08:59,506 --> 00:09:02,032
And that means
somebody like me has to file

143
00:09:02,208 --> 00:09:05,076
what's called an
extraordinary motion.

144
00:09:05,845 --> 00:09:10,545
And I can't do that unless I have
concrete proof that you're innocent.

145
00:09:10,950 --> 00:09:14,011
So if the truth is something
other than you told that jury,

146
00:09:14,187 --> 00:09:16,486
I've got to hear it right now.

147
00:09:20,894 --> 00:09:22,522
Well, I wanted
to tell the truth,

148
00:09:22,695 --> 00:09:24,857
but that shyster lawyer
of mine wouldn't let me.

149
00:09:25,031 --> 00:09:26,124
Why not?

150
00:09:26,299 --> 00:09:28,791
Well, first I kind of
denied everything

151
00:09:28,968 --> 00:09:33,736
and said I was never in the
house. I said I didn't know the victim.

152
00:09:34,607 --> 00:09:37,406
- I lied out of...
- Habit?

153
00:09:41,147 --> 00:09:44,242
But that, uh, lawyer of mine said
that if I suddenly changed my story,

154
00:09:44,417 --> 00:09:47,080
what little credibility I did
have would be shot to hell.

155
00:09:47,520 --> 00:09:49,716
So you were in
that house that night.

156
00:09:52,725 --> 00:09:57,356
Well, earlier that day I was in this
pawnshop a friend of mine owns,

157
00:09:57,530 --> 00:10:01,467
and pretty soon this, uh, woman
comes in with some jewelry.

158
00:10:01,634 --> 00:10:04,661
Family heirlooms,
uh, really nice stuff.

159
00:10:04,971 --> 00:10:09,033
Well, she didn't like what he said
he was gonna give her for it and left.

160
00:10:09,442 --> 00:10:13,436
So I followed her, and turns
out she lived just up the street.

161
00:10:13,613 --> 00:10:15,081
Why'd you follow her?

162
00:10:15,248 --> 00:10:17,376
I was sort of scoping her out.

163
00:10:17,550 --> 00:10:20,145
I figured she lived alone and
a little burglary was in order.

164
00:10:20,320 --> 00:10:22,755
So I cased her
house, uh, real good,

165
00:10:22,922 --> 00:10:25,050
uh, got some tools, came back,

166
00:10:25,225 --> 00:10:28,161
waited for her to leave, and
when she did, I went to work.

167
00:10:28,328 --> 00:10:29,853
How'd you get in?

168
00:10:30,029 --> 00:10:31,622
Just picked the lock
on the front door.

169
00:10:33,600 --> 00:10:36,832
So you were the one the witness
saw in the house that night.

170
00:10:37,003 --> 00:10:38,835
Well, then, yeah, but not later.

171
00:10:39,005 --> 00:10:41,474
I swear, I made a
beeline to the bedroom,

172
00:10:41,641 --> 00:10:44,236
and when I didn't find any
jewelry in the jewelry box,

173
00:10:44,410 --> 00:10:46,709
I looked around the
closet for some kind of safe.

174
00:10:46,880 --> 00:10:48,371
Sure enough, I found it.

175
00:10:49,549 --> 00:10:50,642
You wear gloves?

176
00:10:51,684 --> 00:10:53,118
Of course.

177
00:10:53,453 --> 00:10:55,285
Anyway, um,

178
00:10:55,455 --> 00:10:57,981
I remembered that there
was this key in the jewelry box.

179
00:10:58,158 --> 00:11:00,957
So I tried it in the
safe, and bingo.

180
00:11:01,127 --> 00:11:02,857
I cleaned out everything
that was in there,

181
00:11:03,029 --> 00:11:04,793
waltzed right back
out the front door

182
00:11:04,964 --> 00:11:07,524
and went straight to my
friend, the pawnbroker.

183
00:11:07,700 --> 00:11:10,602
See, he's also a
fence. Little-known fact.

184
00:11:11,004 --> 00:11:12,666
You fenced the
jewelry that night.

185
00:11:13,640 --> 00:11:16,007
That's where I was at
the time of the murder.

186
00:11:16,176 --> 00:11:17,735
Fencing the jewelry.

187
00:11:17,911 --> 00:11:20,676
Anybody...? Anybody
with you that night?

188
00:11:21,714 --> 00:11:23,376
Just him and me.

189
00:11:24,751 --> 00:11:26,583
What's his address?

190
00:11:27,487 --> 00:11:30,946
The pawn shop's in the
2300 block of East Monroe.

191
00:11:31,124 --> 00:11:33,684
Oliver Serling.

192
00:11:35,562 --> 00:11:38,691
Well, I'll give it a shot.

193
00:11:41,568 --> 00:11:43,298
Mr. Matlock,

194
00:11:44,671 --> 00:11:46,299
I'm counting on you.

195
00:11:58,918 --> 00:11:59,942
Trial's over.

196
00:12:00,353 --> 00:12:03,118
With new evidence, I
can get him a new trial.

197
00:12:03,289 --> 00:12:06,350
Can you guarantee my
testimony will get him off?

198
00:12:06,893 --> 00:12:09,453
No, not your
testimony alone, no.

199
00:12:09,629 --> 00:12:12,622
But if you could help us recover
some of this stolen jewelry,

200
00:12:12,799 --> 00:12:15,894
even one piece, I can
almost guarantee it.

201
00:12:18,238 --> 00:12:20,730
I run a respectable
business here.

202
00:12:20,907 --> 00:12:24,036
Most of my clients are overextended
yuppies who bring me their Rolexes

203
00:12:24,210 --> 00:12:25,890
and their LeRoy Neimans
and their Wedgwood,

204
00:12:26,045 --> 00:12:28,344
because they know I'm
gonna give them a fair shake.

205
00:12:28,514 --> 00:12:31,678
Now, if I suddenly copped to
receiving stolen goods, I'm finished,

206
00:12:31,851 --> 00:12:33,717
kaput, through.

207
00:12:35,021 --> 00:12:38,856
If you don't cop to it,
Adam Spangler is dead.

208
00:12:42,228 --> 00:12:45,494
Mr. Serling, according
to Irene Payson,

209
00:12:45,665 --> 00:12:48,134
who witnessed the
murder, and Dick Osmond,

210
00:12:48,301 --> 00:12:50,429
the 911 operator
who took her call

211
00:12:50,603 --> 00:12:52,333
while the murder
was being committed,

212
00:12:52,505 --> 00:12:58,240
the victim died at 11 minutes
after 10, May 21, 1988.

213
00:12:58,411 --> 00:12:59,955
Where were you at
that time? You recall?

214
00:12:59,979 --> 00:13:01,845
I was in the pawn shop I own.

215
00:13:02,015 --> 00:13:03,176
Doing what?

216
00:13:03,349 --> 00:13:05,580
Buying several pieces of jewelry

217
00:13:06,452 --> 00:13:09,217
- from Adam Spangler.
- You sure?

218
00:13:09,389 --> 00:13:11,051
I'm positive.

219
00:13:14,894 --> 00:13:18,422
Was this one of the
items you bought?

220
00:13:18,998 --> 00:13:21,490
That's why I remember
where I was that night.

221
00:13:21,668 --> 00:13:24,536
I liked that piece so much, I kept it.
- Ah.

222
00:13:24,704 --> 00:13:27,333
Had you ever seen
it before that night?

223
00:13:27,507 --> 00:13:30,807
A lady came in that afternoon
wanting to pawn it and some other stuff.

224
00:13:31,277 --> 00:13:33,371
We couldn't come
to terms, so she left.

225
00:13:33,546 --> 00:13:35,412
Did you ever see the lady again?

226
00:13:36,349 --> 00:13:37,942
Not alive, no.

227
00:13:38,117 --> 00:13:39,483
Ah.

228
00:13:40,486 --> 00:13:41,647
That's pretty.

229
00:13:42,288 --> 00:13:45,190
Is it valuable? SERLING: Very.

230
00:13:45,625 --> 00:13:49,756
I had it authenticated, it's
from the 1920s. It's a scarab.

231
00:13:49,929 --> 00:13:51,955
A lot of Egyptian-themed
pieces like that

232
00:13:52,131 --> 00:13:56,296
were made right after King Tut's
tomb was discovered in 1922.

233
00:13:56,869 --> 00:14:00,033
These are some, uh, photos
that were taken by the company

234
00:14:00,206 --> 00:14:04,769
that insured the victim's
jewelry prior to her death.

235
00:14:04,944 --> 00:14:07,175
Do you recognize these photos?

236
00:14:07,947 --> 00:14:08,971
Well, there's the scarab,

237
00:14:09,148 --> 00:14:12,641
and, uh, heh, I
can't be positive,

238
00:14:12,819 --> 00:14:15,880
but I think I may have bought a
couple of those other pieces that night.

239
00:14:16,055 --> 00:14:18,300
What happened to them?
- When I read the paper,

240
00:14:18,324 --> 00:14:20,569
I saw that the lady who had
been in the store was murdered,

241
00:14:20,593 --> 00:14:22,653
I figured the jewelry
was too hot to handle,

242
00:14:22,829 --> 00:14:27,392
so I took out the stones and
melted it down and sold it off that way.

243
00:14:27,567 --> 00:14:30,696
- But you kept the scarab?
- I didn't have the heart to destroy it.

244
00:14:30,870 --> 00:14:33,669
One more time. Are you sure

245
00:14:33,840 --> 00:14:35,809
the defendant, Adam Spangler,

246
00:14:35,975 --> 00:14:39,468
was with you at 11 minutes
after 10 the night of the murder?

247
00:14:39,645 --> 00:14:41,671
Absolutely.

248
00:14:41,848 --> 00:14:44,750
You see, he caught me
just as I was about to lock up,

249
00:14:44,917 --> 00:14:50,379
which I always do at exactly 10 p.m.,
and he stayed for a good half hour.

250
00:14:50,556 --> 00:14:51,990
Thank you.

251
00:14:53,559 --> 00:14:54,959
Miss Harrington.

252
00:14:55,128 --> 00:14:56,255
Rebuttal witnesses?

253
00:14:56,429 --> 00:14:57,573
Thank you, Your Honor.

254
00:14:57,597 --> 00:15:00,897
Just one. The state calls
Mr. Donald Beach to the stand.

255
00:15:08,508 --> 00:15:10,636
Yes, I am a certified gemologist

256
00:15:10,810 --> 00:15:12,779
and metallurgist,

257
00:15:12,945 --> 00:15:16,905
as well as curator of the Jewelry
Division of the Museum of History.

258
00:15:17,083 --> 00:15:19,712
And you can tell if that scarab
is genuine just by looping it?

259
00:15:19,886 --> 00:15:22,082
No, no. By assaying the gold.

260
00:15:22,255 --> 00:15:24,815
You see, pure gold is very soft.

261
00:15:24,991 --> 00:15:28,826
It must be mixed with an alloy
before it can be used to make jewelry.

262
00:15:28,995 --> 00:15:32,363
Different alloys have been
used in different decades.

263
00:15:32,532 --> 00:15:35,934
If this scarab was
indeed made in the 1920s,

264
00:15:36,102 --> 00:15:38,970
then a drop of this chemical
will react with the gold

265
00:15:39,138 --> 00:15:41,664
to produce no foam, nothing.

266
00:15:41,841 --> 00:15:44,572
If there is foam,
this jewelry is fake.

267
00:15:44,744 --> 00:15:46,838
Objection. Irrelevant.

268
00:15:47,013 --> 00:15:49,278
Fake or real, that's
still the same brooch

269
00:15:49,449 --> 00:15:52,283
my client was selling Mr. Serling
at the time of the murder.

270
00:15:52,452 --> 00:15:56,822
Mr. Serling testified under oath
that he had this piece authenticated.

271
00:15:56,989 --> 00:15:58,685
Now, if it turns
out to be a fake,

272
00:15:58,858 --> 00:16:03,353
it means he not only makes
money illegally, but he's a liar as well.

273
00:16:03,529 --> 00:16:06,522
- Objection overruled.
- Go ahead, Mr. Beach.

274
00:16:06,699 --> 00:16:09,498
Very well, I'll place
a drop right here.

275
00:16:14,707 --> 00:16:17,802
There's no way this
jewelry was made in the '20s.

276
00:16:18,277 --> 00:16:23,409
The very earliest the alloys in this
gold were used was in the late 1960s.

277
00:16:24,450 --> 00:16:27,716
My guess is that
this reproduction, hm,

278
00:16:28,321 --> 00:16:31,883
as good as it is,
was made recently.

279
00:16:32,925 --> 00:16:36,293
So much for Mr. Serling's
testimony. Thank you.

280
00:16:47,640 --> 00:16:50,576
This is a first for
me, you know that?

281
00:16:51,177 --> 00:16:53,942
I wish I'd never
laid eyes on you.

282
00:16:54,413 --> 00:16:56,507
You're the first
person I ever heard of

283
00:16:56,682 --> 00:17:01,143
to get the death penalty
twice for the same crime.

284
00:17:02,121 --> 00:17:04,317
- I'm still innocent.
- Innocent.

285
00:17:04,490 --> 00:17:07,255
You manufactured evidence,
and then you got somebody

286
00:17:07,426 --> 00:17:11,363
even dumber than you are to
commit perjury. You call that innocent?

287
00:17:11,531 --> 00:17:14,296
You must really have
something on that guy.

288
00:17:14,467 --> 00:17:16,436
I don't want to know about it.

289
00:17:18,070 --> 00:17:20,301
I didn't kill that woman.

290
00:17:22,241 --> 00:17:27,202
You would say or do
anything to save your skin.

291
00:17:28,714 --> 00:17:30,444
Well, wouldn't you?

292
00:17:31,150 --> 00:17:34,279
You tell me my only chance
of getting off is to find evidence.

293
00:17:36,455 --> 00:17:38,014
There isn't any.

294
00:17:38,190 --> 00:17:41,354
So I made it up. It's not
like I have a whole lot to lose.

295
00:17:41,761 --> 00:17:43,821
Gonna lose me.

296
00:17:45,331 --> 00:17:46,731
You don't even
know what happened.

297
00:17:46,899 --> 00:17:49,459
You can't tell the truth.

298
00:17:53,172 --> 00:17:55,198
God's honest truth.

299
00:18:04,483 --> 00:18:08,079
I saw that woman at the
pawnshop and I followed her home.

300
00:18:08,254 --> 00:18:11,224
I cased the place,
got some tools,

301
00:18:11,390 --> 00:18:13,621
waited for her to leave,
and when she did,

302
00:18:13,793 --> 00:18:17,230
I picked the lock, went
inside, looked around.

303
00:18:18,064 --> 00:18:22,801
I found the safe, but
I didn't find no key.

304
00:18:23,436 --> 00:18:24,927
So I left.

305
00:18:25,838 --> 00:18:27,238
You couldn't pick it?

306
00:18:29,809 --> 00:18:31,004
No.

307
00:18:31,677 --> 00:18:33,703
You didn't take anything?

308
00:18:33,879 --> 00:18:34,919
There was nothing to take.

309
00:18:35,081 --> 00:18:36,881
All the valuables must
have been in that safe.

310
00:18:37,016 --> 00:18:38,814
Believe me, I looked.

311
00:18:41,420 --> 00:18:44,652
Before, you told me
you took the jewels.

312
00:18:45,858 --> 00:18:47,326
I lied.

313
00:18:48,227 --> 00:18:50,196
I got nothing.

314
00:18:51,530 --> 00:18:53,829
Afterwards, I hit
a couple of bars.

315
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,265
Around midnight I
ended up at Roy's Tavern.

316
00:18:56,435 --> 00:18:59,837
I sold my tools to some kids
and went home with Beth.

317
00:19:00,006 --> 00:19:02,532
She was my girlfriend at
the time. She tends bar there.

318
00:19:03,242 --> 00:19:04,710
Why'd you sell the tools?

319
00:19:05,144 --> 00:19:07,773
SOP. I travel light
and low-profile.

320
00:19:07,947 --> 00:19:12,476
No guns, no drugs,
no contraband, never.

321
00:19:13,085 --> 00:19:14,519
When did you get the tools?

322
00:19:14,687 --> 00:19:17,816
That night. I bought them on the
street right before I went to the house.

323
00:19:19,158 --> 00:19:22,890
Somebody must have been right
there and gone in right after I left.

324
00:19:23,229 --> 00:19:25,323
That's all I can figure.

325
00:19:28,868 --> 00:19:31,838
So do you believe me?

326
00:19:40,513 --> 00:19:42,004
Ben.

327
00:20:00,132 --> 00:20:02,124
I'm sorry for dragging
you into this, Ben.

328
00:20:02,301 --> 00:20:03,530
He's an ungrateful, two-faced,

329
00:20:03,703 --> 00:20:06,901
lying little snake who's
about to get what he deserves.

330
00:20:07,073 --> 00:20:08,564
I guess.

331
00:20:10,242 --> 00:20:13,610
He said somebody must
have come in after he left.

332
00:20:13,779 --> 00:20:15,372
He's lying.

333
00:20:17,349 --> 00:20:19,875
Might explain that
broken window.

334
00:20:20,052 --> 00:20:22,578
He probably broke
it just to throw us off.

335
00:20:22,755 --> 00:20:24,656
Could explain that set of prints

336
00:20:24,824 --> 00:20:26,315
that have never been identified.

337
00:20:26,492 --> 00:20:29,826
Probably belonged to the
victim's long-lost Uncle Harry.

338
00:20:29,995 --> 00:20:31,827
Forget about him.

339
00:20:31,997 --> 00:20:34,159
- Come on. I'll buy you a drink.
- Drink?

340
00:20:34,333 --> 00:20:36,859
You're buying me a
seven-course dinner.

341
00:20:37,036 --> 00:20:40,905
Let's just make one little
stop so I can sleep tonight.

342
00:20:43,743 --> 00:20:45,143
- Please come in.
- Thank you.

343
00:20:45,277 --> 00:20:47,109
Detective Bouchard,
how nice to see you aga...

344
00:20:48,481 --> 00:20:50,814
- Don't tell me you still smoke.
- Afraid so.

345
00:20:50,983 --> 00:20:53,475
Well, don't even think
of lighting up in here.

346
00:20:53,652 --> 00:20:57,054
Your clothes alone are enough
to make me start sneezing.

347
00:20:57,223 --> 00:20:58,816
Yes, ma'am. Sorry.

348
00:20:58,991 --> 00:21:01,324
Oh, sit down, please.

349
00:21:02,061 --> 00:21:04,690
Well, would you,
uh, like some tea?

350
00:21:04,864 --> 00:21:08,062
- Uh, no, thanks. I'm fine.
- Me too.

351
00:21:08,534 --> 00:21:11,834
Not that I mind the company, but I
should think you would have come by

352
00:21:12,004 --> 00:21:13,563
before the trial, not after.

353
00:21:13,739 --> 00:21:18,109
Well, the fact is, I was
sure I could get an acquittal

354
00:21:18,277 --> 00:21:20,405
based on what I thought
was new evidence.

355
00:21:20,579 --> 00:21:23,879
Now I guess I'm just
trying to ease my mind.

356
00:21:24,984 --> 00:21:27,112
I know what that's like.

357
00:21:28,020 --> 00:21:31,422
If I'd known that my testimony was
going to send that man to his death,

358
00:21:31,590 --> 00:21:34,082
I would never have
opened my mouth.

359
00:21:34,593 --> 00:21:35,891
What he did was horrible,

360
00:21:36,061 --> 00:21:38,690
but turning around and
doing the same thing to him

361
00:21:38,864 --> 00:21:40,492
makes us no better than him.

362
00:21:41,867 --> 00:21:44,735
Now, before you
and old Campbell here

363
00:21:44,904 --> 00:21:46,930
start going round
and round on that,

364
00:21:47,106 --> 00:21:49,905
tell me again what
happened that night.

365
00:21:50,075 --> 00:21:53,341
What you saw, where
you were when you saw it.

366
00:21:53,512 --> 00:21:54,810
Well,

367
00:21:57,583 --> 00:22:01,350
I was, uh... I was
doing my laundry,

368
00:22:01,520 --> 00:22:03,921
downstairs, like I do
every Wednesday night.

369
00:22:04,089 --> 00:22:05,580
And then I came up here.

370
00:22:05,758 --> 00:22:07,989
It was about a quarter of 10.

371
00:22:08,160 --> 00:22:09,685
It was real muggy outside,

372
00:22:09,862 --> 00:22:15,665
so the first thing I did was walk
over to this window and open it.

373
00:22:16,669 --> 00:22:20,333
And that's when I
saw Adam Spangler

374
00:22:20,940 --> 00:22:25,036
coming up the front walk
to the house next door.

375
00:22:25,744 --> 00:22:27,872
You're sure it was him,
even though it was dark?

376
00:22:28,047 --> 00:22:30,846
Oh, yes, the porch
lights were on.

377
00:22:31,016 --> 00:22:33,713
I got a real good look
at him at the front door.

378
00:22:33,886 --> 00:22:38,551
But he was so nonchalant
about it, I didn't think anything of it.

379
00:22:38,724 --> 00:22:43,560
So he went into the house, and then
I went on about my business up here.

380
00:22:43,729 --> 00:22:45,288
Doing what?

381
00:22:45,698 --> 00:22:47,894
Well, let's see, I, um...

382
00:22:48,067 --> 00:22:51,435
I put away the
laundry, I ran my bath,

383
00:22:51,604 --> 00:22:53,596
I made some tea.

384
00:22:53,772 --> 00:22:55,934
And I didn't even look
out the window again

385
00:22:56,108 --> 00:23:00,170
until I went to close it
about 20 minutes later.

386
00:23:01,513 --> 00:23:03,573
And that's when I saw

387
00:23:04,650 --> 00:23:07,119
that he was in her bedroom,

388
00:23:07,286 --> 00:23:09,653
just tearing everything apart.

389
00:23:11,023 --> 00:23:13,151
You're sure it was the same man?

390
00:23:13,826 --> 00:23:16,159
Oh, absolutely.

391
00:23:17,062 --> 00:23:19,156
The light was on.

392
00:23:19,331 --> 00:23:21,357
I saw him plain as day.

393
00:23:21,533 --> 00:23:25,698
He was pulling out the
drawers and rifling through them

394
00:23:26,305 --> 00:23:28,399
and just tossing them aside.

395
00:23:28,908 --> 00:23:32,003
And then all of a sudden, he
stopped like he'd heard something,

396
00:23:32,177 --> 00:23:34,510
and he ran toward
the light switch.

397
00:23:34,680 --> 00:23:36,842
No sooner had the
lights gone off in there

398
00:23:37,016 --> 00:23:40,214
than a lamp came
on in the front room,

399
00:23:40,386 --> 00:23:44,687
and I suddenly realized that
while I'd been watching him,

400
00:23:44,857 --> 00:23:48,453
the woman who owned
the house had come home.

401
00:23:48,627 --> 00:23:50,323
I didn't know what to do,

402
00:23:50,496 --> 00:23:53,193
but she went out of sight

403
00:23:53,365 --> 00:23:55,925
into the back of the house.

404
00:23:57,970 --> 00:23:59,563
And then

405
00:24:00,039 --> 00:24:02,304
the bedroom lights
went on again.

406
00:24:03,375 --> 00:24:07,039
And I saw her walk through
the door and stop suddenly,

407
00:24:08,714 --> 00:24:11,206
because there he was,

408
00:24:11,784 --> 00:24:14,413
with a gun pointed at her.

409
00:24:14,853 --> 00:24:16,048
Was he wearing gloves?

410
00:24:16,221 --> 00:24:18,554
I don't think so, no.

411
00:24:18,724 --> 00:24:23,059
And then I saw him
say something to her,

412
00:24:24,363 --> 00:24:26,923
and she said something to him.

413
00:24:28,100 --> 00:24:31,628
And then she held
out her key ring.

414
00:24:32,738 --> 00:24:35,640
He... He grabbed it from her,

415
00:24:35,874 --> 00:24:39,940
took a step back, raised the gun

416
00:24:41,347 --> 00:24:43,316
and just shot her.

417
00:24:45,551 --> 00:24:47,713
And then he went
into the closet,

418
00:24:49,989 --> 00:24:53,153
without even giving
her a second glance.

419
00:24:54,126 --> 00:25:00,362
By that time, I'd reached 911 and I
was crying hysterically at the operator.

420
00:25:00,833 --> 00:25:02,802
He came out of the closet

421
00:25:02,968 --> 00:25:05,904
and he was holding a
lot of jewelry in his hands,

422
00:25:06,572 --> 00:25:08,302
and then he ran out of the room.

423
00:25:08,474 --> 00:25:10,136
And there's not a
doubt in your mind

424
00:25:10,309 --> 00:25:13,074
the man you saw that
night was Adam Spangler?

425
00:25:14,880 --> 00:25:16,906
I wish there was.

426
00:25:18,150 --> 00:25:20,312
But God help me, no.

427
00:25:21,320 --> 00:25:22,344
There's not.

428
00:25:26,258 --> 00:25:28,090
It was him.

429
00:25:30,396 --> 00:25:31,591
You happy?

430
00:25:31,764 --> 00:25:34,393
No, but I guess I'm satisfied.

431
00:25:35,134 --> 00:25:37,694
And real hungry.

432
00:25:54,586 --> 00:25:57,351
I'm coming. Oh!

433
00:25:59,124 --> 00:26:00,558
Yeah, I'm coming.

434
00:26:05,364 --> 00:26:07,128
- Ah.
- Hi, Ben.

435
00:26:07,833 --> 00:26:08,857
Campbell Bouchard.

436
00:26:09,034 --> 00:26:11,526
Yeah, it's been a while,
hasn't it? How you doing?

437
00:26:11,703 --> 00:26:15,936
I never do good at 4:00 in the
morning. Put that cigarette out.

438
00:26:16,108 --> 00:26:18,839
Listen, I'm sorry, but I
figured you'd want to hear this.

439
00:26:19,011 --> 00:26:21,242
- What?
- A home out in Hillsdale

440
00:26:21,413 --> 00:26:24,042
was burglarized tonight. Somebody
broke the downstairs window,

441
00:26:24,216 --> 00:26:26,048
entered the house,
stole a bunch of jewelry

442
00:26:26,218 --> 00:26:28,418
and climbed back out the
same window without being seen.

443
00:26:28,954 --> 00:26:31,253
- Can I go back to bed now?
- Wait a minute.

444
00:26:32,391 --> 00:26:35,953
Remember that set of fingerprints
we found in the Adam Spangler case,

445
00:26:36,128 --> 00:26:38,256
- the ones we never could account for?
- Yeah.

446
00:26:38,430 --> 00:26:43,869
Ben, we found that same set of
prints at the house in Hillsdale tonight.

447
00:26:48,207 --> 00:26:49,231
No.

448
00:26:49,942 --> 00:26:51,410
Yes.

449
00:26:52,311 --> 00:26:54,280
Adam Spangler is innocent.

450
00:27:11,330 --> 00:27:13,822
Hello, Ben. Long time no see.

451
00:27:16,001 --> 00:27:17,469
Who's this pretty lady?

452
00:27:17,636 --> 00:27:20,936
This is my daughter, Leanne
Mclntyre. She's a lawyer too.

453
00:27:21,106 --> 00:27:23,075
We've been partners
for over a year.

454
00:27:23,242 --> 00:27:24,835
Well, then that must mean

455
00:27:25,010 --> 00:27:27,673
you're every bit as talented
as you are beautiful. Heh-heh.

456
00:27:27,846 --> 00:27:30,714
And you are every bit as
full of it as Dad said you'd be.

457
00:27:34,553 --> 00:27:38,116
Losing my touch,
heh, that's all.

458
00:27:39,191 --> 00:27:41,820
Three years on, uh, death
row will do that to you.

459
00:27:42,861 --> 00:27:44,921
So, what's this
about new evidence?

460
00:27:45,097 --> 00:27:48,966
A home out in Hillsdale
was burglarized last night.

461
00:27:49,134 --> 00:27:51,535
Police lifted a set of prints

462
00:27:51,703 --> 00:27:54,263
that matched those they
could never identify in your case.

463
00:27:56,375 --> 00:27:58,935
Then there was somebody
there that night after I left.

464
00:27:59,545 --> 00:28:02,105
Well, have they found him
yet? Do they know who he is?

465
00:28:02,281 --> 00:28:04,682
The prints are not
on file anywhere.

466
00:28:05,417 --> 00:28:07,943
They were a partial set,
just like the set in your case,

467
00:28:08,120 --> 00:28:10,680
which leads the
police to believe

468
00:28:10,856 --> 00:28:13,291
that this guy may be
missing a couple of fingers.

469
00:28:13,825 --> 00:28:14,952
So, what happens next?

470
00:28:15,127 --> 00:28:18,097
Keep filing appeals
till he's found.

471
00:28:20,165 --> 00:28:21,997
More appeals.

472
00:28:22,768 --> 00:28:25,169
Ah, Ben, they keep
getting rejected.

473
00:28:25,771 --> 00:28:27,069
Look at it this way,

474
00:28:27,239 --> 00:28:28,468
they're keeping you alive.

475
00:28:29,541 --> 00:28:33,342
You know, the fact that
somebody went in that house

476
00:28:33,512 --> 00:28:36,107
after you came out is
too much of a coincidence

477
00:28:36,281 --> 00:28:38,773
for it to be a random burglary.

478
00:28:38,951 --> 00:28:41,511
Somebody must have
known about those jewels,

479
00:28:41,687 --> 00:28:45,454
and maybe he was
planning to rob you,

480
00:28:45,624 --> 00:28:46,990
like you robbed that woman.

481
00:28:47,793 --> 00:28:50,558
When he saw you come out
of the house empty-handed,

482
00:28:50,729 --> 00:28:55,497
maybe he figured he'd
go in and have better luck.

483
00:28:55,667 --> 00:28:57,397
Who knew you were
gonna steal that jewelry?

484
00:28:59,204 --> 00:29:00,934
Let's see...

485
00:29:04,409 --> 00:29:07,174
Well, I told Serling, because
I wanted to make sure

486
00:29:07,346 --> 00:29:11,078
that he'd buy the stuff
once I got it. And, uh, Beth.

487
00:29:11,516 --> 00:29:14,384
Beth Newton. She was
my girlfriend at the time.

488
00:29:14,720 --> 00:29:16,586
In fact, she set
me up with the guy

489
00:29:16,755 --> 00:29:19,281
I bought the tools from
that I used that night.

490
00:29:20,826 --> 00:29:22,021
What's his name?

491
00:29:23,395 --> 00:29:24,829
Hock.

492
00:29:25,664 --> 00:29:26,893
Don't know his real name.

493
00:29:27,065 --> 00:29:28,499
What does he look like?

494
00:29:28,667 --> 00:29:31,034
About my height, medium build.

495
00:29:32,904 --> 00:29:36,602
I met him under the pier at
the amusement park that night.

496
00:29:36,775 --> 00:29:39,370
It was dark. I didn't
get a good look at him.

497
00:29:41,246 --> 00:29:45,049
He was smoking a
cigar, I do remember that.

498
00:29:45,217 --> 00:29:49,712
So Serling and Beth and Hock

499
00:29:49,888 --> 00:29:51,948
knew what you were
planning to do that night.

500
00:29:52,124 --> 00:29:53,319
Well, it couldn't be Serling.

501
00:29:53,492 --> 00:29:55,859
He was gonna end up
with the stuff anyway.

502
00:29:56,662 --> 00:29:57,857
It's gotta be this Hock guy.

503
00:29:59,498 --> 00:30:01,057
Time's up.

504
00:30:01,233 --> 00:30:04,203
Uh, Beth might
have told somebody.

505
00:30:04,369 --> 00:30:07,168
Beth wouldn't have
double-crossed me like that.

506
00:30:08,774 --> 00:30:10,242
She loved me.

507
00:30:11,510 --> 00:30:14,674
I don't understand
why, but she did.

508
00:30:23,789 --> 00:30:27,055
The worse he treated
me, the more I wanted him.

509
00:30:27,225 --> 00:30:30,627
I was like a piece of gum
stuck to the bottom of his shoe.

510
00:30:30,796 --> 00:30:33,197
I was always there,

511
00:30:34,266 --> 00:30:38,260
until finally one night I
heard this knock on my door,

512
00:30:38,437 --> 00:30:40,633
and there he was.

513
00:30:42,474 --> 00:30:45,137
I'd worn him down. Heh.

514
00:30:46,645 --> 00:30:49,376
He didn't move out until
they sent him to prison.

515
00:30:51,083 --> 00:30:52,984
It hurts thinking about him.

516
00:30:53,151 --> 00:30:56,121
I wouldn't ask unless
I thought it would help.

517
00:30:58,357 --> 00:30:59,877
See that guy in
the blue T-shirt?

518
00:31:00,492 --> 00:31:02,688
That's Patty McCord.

519
00:31:03,295 --> 00:31:05,924
He and my brother were Marines.

520
00:31:06,098 --> 00:31:08,590
Patty lost the use of his
legs and my brother died

521
00:31:08,767 --> 00:31:11,999
when their barracks were
bombed by terrorists in Lebanon.

522
00:31:12,170 --> 00:31:13,365
Shot's up!

523
00:31:13,939 --> 00:31:17,171
Patty promised my brother
that he'd watch over me.

524
00:31:18,810 --> 00:31:22,611
He's a really nice guy. He
gets along with everybody.

525
00:31:22,781 --> 00:31:24,443
He has a lot of
connections on the street.

526
00:31:24,616 --> 00:31:27,484
Like when I told him Adam
needed some tools for that night,

527
00:31:27,652 --> 00:31:31,646
he just said no problem, and
arranged the meeting under the pier.

528
00:31:32,457 --> 00:31:34,255
So Hock's his friend, not yours.

529
00:31:34,426 --> 00:31:37,157
I wouldn't know
Hock if I fell over him.

530
00:31:38,230 --> 00:31:39,664
Ball!

531
00:31:40,065 --> 00:31:41,829
Hey!

532
00:31:47,172 --> 00:31:48,731
Patty.

533
00:31:49,508 --> 00:31:51,136
Hi, Beth.

534
00:31:51,443 --> 00:31:53,207
Out on the wing, I'm over here.

535
00:31:53,378 --> 00:31:56,212
Hey. Did you see that shot?

536
00:31:56,381 --> 00:31:57,872
Nothing but net.

537
00:31:58,049 --> 00:32:00,143
Ah. Guess it can't be the shoes.

538
00:32:00,318 --> 00:32:02,981
No wonder I don't
get any endorsements.

539
00:32:03,288 --> 00:32:05,757
- Patty, this is Leanne Mclntyre.
- Hi.

540
00:32:05,924 --> 00:32:07,017
How you doing?

541
00:32:07,192 --> 00:32:08,956
She's one of Adam
Spangler's lawyers.

542
00:32:09,528 --> 00:32:12,555
She needs to talk
to you about Hock.

543
00:32:12,731 --> 00:32:15,098
Come on, Beth, you
know better than that.

544
00:32:15,667 --> 00:32:17,533
Patty, please, it's important.

545
00:32:17,702 --> 00:32:19,671
- Forget it.
- Mr. McCord, I just want to talk.

546
00:32:19,838 --> 00:32:21,170
It could save his life.

547
00:32:21,873 --> 00:32:23,034
No way.

548
00:32:23,475 --> 00:32:25,410
Patty, wait.

549
00:32:35,220 --> 00:32:37,382
Mail's here. MATLOCK: Mm.

550
00:32:45,630 --> 00:32:46,962
Dad,

551
00:32:49,034 --> 00:32:50,593
did you get a computer?

552
00:32:51,002 --> 00:32:52,664
Where's the monitor?

553
00:32:53,171 --> 00:32:54,264
Don't use it.

554
00:32:54,439 --> 00:32:56,772
- Why not?
- It doesn't like me.

555
00:33:07,285 --> 00:33:09,447
I don't roll over on people.

556
00:33:09,621 --> 00:33:11,487
It's a thing with me.

557
00:33:12,858 --> 00:33:15,020
Her brother was my best friend.

558
00:33:15,794 --> 00:33:18,229
But this is important to her.

559
00:33:20,499 --> 00:33:22,263
What do you want to know?

560
00:33:22,467 --> 00:33:23,526
Who is this guy, Hock?

561
00:33:23,702 --> 00:33:28,936
He's a thief. Minor league, breaking
and entering, a couple of car heists.

562
00:33:29,307 --> 00:33:30,570
Never been caught.

563
00:33:30,742 --> 00:33:33,974
He thinks that's really cool,
considering he's more or less disabled.

564
00:33:34,145 --> 00:33:35,443
What do you mean?

565
00:33:35,614 --> 00:33:37,734
McCORD: He's only got
three fingers on his right hand.

566
00:33:38,016 --> 00:33:41,111
Blew them off playing with
cherry bombs when he was a kid.

567
00:33:41,786 --> 00:33:42,981
Where can we find him?

568
00:33:45,957 --> 00:33:48,927
Patty, please?

569
00:33:52,831 --> 00:33:54,356
Please?

570
00:34:16,488 --> 00:34:18,980
Oh, there's Detectives
Harris and Lieberman.

571
00:34:22,427 --> 00:34:26,956
There's Detective O'Doyle
and there's Campbell.

572
00:34:27,132 --> 00:34:28,760
Yeah, but where's Hock?

573
00:34:28,934 --> 00:34:30,493
Any minute now.

574
00:34:30,669 --> 00:34:33,366
What if this is another one
of Adam Spangler's scams?

575
00:34:33,538 --> 00:34:35,973
I mean, we both know he's
not much for telling the truth.

576
00:34:36,141 --> 00:34:40,203
What if he got Beth to get Patty
to make up this whole Hock thing?

577
00:34:40,378 --> 00:34:42,313
Now, if he did,

578
00:34:42,480 --> 00:34:44,972
I'll save the state some
money and kill him myself.

579
00:34:48,520 --> 00:34:49,749
What?

580
00:34:50,255 --> 00:34:52,019
That guy over there.

581
00:34:52,190 --> 00:34:54,352
He's the right size, height,

582
00:34:54,526 --> 00:34:57,655
wearing gloves,
cigar, got a bag.

583
00:34:57,829 --> 00:34:59,092
It's him.

584
00:34:59,264 --> 00:35:01,199
Yeah, but he's
going the wrong way.

585
00:35:01,366 --> 00:35:04,962
He knows something's up. I'll
follow him, and you get Campbell.

586
00:35:42,974 --> 00:35:44,533
Where is he?

587
00:35:44,709 --> 00:35:46,041
There.

588
00:35:46,945 --> 00:35:49,346
All units, he's heading
for the playground.

589
00:35:50,682 --> 00:35:52,480
Hold it right there!

590
00:36:08,633 --> 00:36:09,931
Sandwiches.

591
00:36:25,884 --> 00:36:27,443
I don't understand.

592
00:36:27,619 --> 00:36:29,850
You mean, there's still a
chance he won't be executed?

593
00:36:30,021 --> 00:36:33,389
There's always a chance, right
up until they throw the switch.

594
00:36:33,558 --> 00:36:36,153
- How much time does he have left?
- That depends.

595
00:36:36,327 --> 00:36:40,423
I'm looking for a reason to file
what's called a writ of <i>habeas corpus,</i>

596
00:36:40,598 --> 00:36:44,763
so I can get the state supreme
court to hear new testimony.

597
00:36:45,537 --> 00:36:47,233
I don't have
anything new to say.

598
00:36:47,872 --> 00:36:52,367
Well, Miss Payson, in thinking
back on what you told me before,

599
00:36:52,544 --> 00:36:56,242
something I couldn't quite understand.
You said it was muggy in here,

600
00:36:56,781 --> 00:36:59,649
and the first thing you did
when you came back up here

601
00:36:59,818 --> 00:37:02,117
was open the window.

602
00:37:02,287 --> 00:37:06,554
Then you said 20 minutes
later, you closed it again. Why?

603
00:37:07,459 --> 00:37:09,792
I don't know why.

604
00:37:11,362 --> 00:37:13,627
Oh. Cigar smoke.

605
00:37:13,798 --> 00:37:18,827
If I don't close this, I'll
have a sneezing fit for su...

606
00:37:20,205 --> 00:37:21,571
What?

607
00:37:23,341 --> 00:37:25,810
I smelled cigar
smoke that night.

608
00:37:25,977 --> 00:37:27,639
That's why I closed the window.

609
00:37:28,713 --> 00:37:32,844
Miss Payson, it's
possible, just possible,

610
00:37:33,017 --> 00:37:36,579
that somebody was standing
down there between the houses,

611
00:37:36,755 --> 00:37:39,418
watching Adam
Spangler enter the house,

612
00:37:39,591 --> 00:37:41,287
just like you were from up here.

613
00:37:41,459 --> 00:37:43,485
And when you walked
away from the window,

614
00:37:43,661 --> 00:37:47,496
he threw his cigar down and
entered the house after Adam had left,

615
00:37:48,199 --> 00:37:52,534
and that's the person who killed
your neighbor. Isn't it possible?

616
00:37:53,638 --> 00:37:57,700
Well, if he looked enough
like Adam, I suppose so.

617
00:37:57,876 --> 00:37:59,708
Yes.

618
00:37:59,878 --> 00:38:03,144
That would explain
the cigar smoke.

619
00:38:05,383 --> 00:38:06,851
Leanne!

620
00:38:07,018 --> 00:38:08,543
We have cause to file a writ.

621
00:38:08,720 --> 00:38:11,246
Put that thing out
and come up here.

622
00:38:16,461 --> 00:38:19,863
You've read the written
declarations I've submitted.

623
00:38:20,031 --> 00:38:21,829
But those are just words.

624
00:38:22,000 --> 00:38:24,128
This case is about emotions.

625
00:38:24,736 --> 00:38:27,262
The emotions of an eyewitness,

626
00:38:27,438 --> 00:38:30,738
the emotions of a jury.

627
00:38:30,909 --> 00:38:35,938
Human memory is a
fragile thing to begin with,

628
00:38:36,114 --> 00:38:40,381
and when enlisted to
recall something as horrible

629
00:38:40,552 --> 00:38:44,785
as the crime of murder, well,
emotions just naturally get in the way.

630
00:38:44,956 --> 00:38:46,925
Some events are heightened,

631
00:38:47,091 --> 00:38:49,583
some suppressed to the point

632
00:38:50,195 --> 00:38:51,561
of being forgotten,

633
00:38:51,729 --> 00:38:55,029
and I submit to you that
that's what happened

634
00:38:55,200 --> 00:38:58,932
in the case of <i>Spangler
v. The State of Georgia.</i>

635
00:38:59,103 --> 00:39:04,064
Adam Spangler is a con man
who tried to con a jury and failed.

636
00:39:04,242 --> 00:39:06,302
Irene Payson is an
eyewitness to murder.

637
00:39:06,477 --> 00:39:10,137
But it so upset
her that later on,

638
00:39:10,315 --> 00:39:12,580
she wasn't so much able to
remember what happened,

639
00:39:12,750 --> 00:39:16,278
as to assume what happened.

640
00:39:16,654 --> 00:39:18,850
The truth of the matter is

641
00:39:19,524 --> 00:39:23,393
the crime for which Adam
Spangler is to be executed

642
00:39:23,561 --> 00:39:25,120
was committed by another man.

643
00:39:25,296 --> 00:39:26,559
A burglar whose fingerprints

644
00:39:26,731 --> 00:39:28,927
were found not only at
the scene of the crime,

645
00:39:29,100 --> 00:39:30,830
but at another burglary as well,

646
00:39:31,002 --> 00:39:34,495
a fact to which Detective
Bouchard will soon attest.

647
00:39:34,672 --> 00:39:36,868
A man who smokes a cigar,

648
00:39:37,041 --> 00:39:41,479
who smoked a cigar outside the
victim's house the night of the murder,

649
00:39:42,113 --> 00:39:45,174
as Irene Payson
will soon attest.

650
00:39:45,350 --> 00:39:50,846
This man whom we
are still trying to seek out

651
00:39:52,056 --> 00:39:53,854
is the real killer.

652
00:39:54,025 --> 00:39:57,962
I've come here to convince
you in the interests of justice

653
00:39:58,129 --> 00:40:02,157
to at least stay the
execution of Adam Spangler

654
00:40:02,333 --> 00:40:04,598
till this man can be found.

655
00:40:05,970 --> 00:40:07,529
To that end, petitioner calls

656
00:40:07,705 --> 00:40:10,766
Detective Campbell
Bouchard to the stand.

657
00:40:12,377 --> 00:40:14,642
That won't be
necessary, Mr. Matlock.

658
00:40:14,812 --> 00:40:17,976
This court was convened to hear
oral arguments, not new testimony.

659
00:40:18,149 --> 00:40:19,777
- But, Your Honor...
- Mr. Matlock,

660
00:40:19,951 --> 00:40:23,012
the declarations that you've made here
haven't brought us any new evidence.

661
00:40:23,187 --> 00:40:26,988
In fact, all you've submitted
here is speculation.

662
00:40:27,158 --> 00:40:31,994
It's not our place or our purpose
to weigh arguments based on such.

663
00:40:33,264 --> 00:40:35,130
Your appeal is denied.

664
00:40:37,001 --> 00:40:39,698
Petitioner, Adam Spangler,

665
00:40:39,871 --> 00:40:41,430
will be put to
death as sentenced

666
00:40:41,606 --> 00:40:46,306
at 2 a.m. two weeks from today.

667
00:40:59,590 --> 00:41:01,923
No. No, we don't
have new evidence.

668
00:41:02,093 --> 00:41:05,222
- Judge Garrison, hi, Leanne Mclntyre.
- But if you could just suggest

669
00:41:05,396 --> 00:41:07,956
to your viewers that they
call their assemblyman...

670
00:41:08,132 --> 00:41:10,397
Well, yes, sir, I'm
sorry, I do know it's late.

671
00:41:10,568 --> 00:41:12,408
I was wondering, could
you consider staying...?

672
00:41:12,570 --> 00:41:14,436
I understand.

673
00:41:14,605 --> 00:41:16,005
Judge?

674
00:41:16,708 --> 00:41:19,735
Oh... Dad, did you hear back
from the U.S. supreme court?

675
00:41:19,911 --> 00:41:21,846
- Refused our petition.
- Oh.

676
00:41:22,013 --> 00:41:24,073
Did you ever hear
back from Patty?

677
00:41:24,248 --> 00:41:26,688
He said he would call us as
soon as he gets the slightest hint

678
00:41:26,818 --> 00:41:28,650
- as to where Hock is.
- Mm.

679
00:41:30,021 --> 00:41:31,581
No time to be playing
with the computer.

680
00:41:31,723 --> 00:41:33,083
I'm not playing
with the computer.

681
00:41:33,257 --> 00:41:36,125
I have the phone number of every
judge in the state of Georgia in here.

682
00:41:36,294 --> 00:41:37,318
Yeah?

683
00:41:39,497 --> 00:41:41,762
- Yeah?
- Ben, it's Campbell.

684
00:41:41,933 --> 00:41:43,413
We just picked up
the second-story man

685
00:41:43,568 --> 00:41:45,127
who might know
something about Hock.

686
00:41:46,604 --> 00:41:47,765
What'd he say?

687
00:41:47,939 --> 00:41:49,805
Nothing yet. Hasn't
been Mirandized.

688
00:41:49,974 --> 00:41:52,569
Where you gonna be in case we
shake something out of this guy?

689
00:41:52,944 --> 00:41:55,345
Leanne will be here. Call her
the second you got anything.

690
00:41:55,513 --> 00:41:57,573
I'll be out at the prison.

691
00:42:35,753 --> 00:42:37,949
There's still a chance.

692
00:42:42,293 --> 00:42:43,784
What's that?

693
00:42:45,897 --> 00:42:49,390
I took Beth up to
Graceland one weekend,

694
00:42:49,567 --> 00:42:54,198
gave my camera to some
guy to take a picture of us.

695
00:42:55,740 --> 00:42:57,732
It's a picture of a bush.

696
00:43:01,946 --> 00:43:04,780
Yeah, he must have
been moving the camera

697
00:43:04,949 --> 00:43:07,111
when he pushed the button.

698
00:43:08,786 --> 00:43:12,723
Yeah, pictures never
did Beth justice, anyway.

699
00:43:13,458 --> 00:43:17,088
She was always
prettier in real life.

700
00:43:38,116 --> 00:43:42,850
I figure this is happening
for a reason, you know?

701
00:43:44,689 --> 00:43:46,282
It's gotta be.

702
00:43:47,959 --> 00:43:50,986
I just wish I knew
what the hell it was.

703
00:43:58,369 --> 00:44:00,804
I understand that, Your Honor.

704
00:44:02,006 --> 00:44:06,569
Yes. Okay, Judge Katz.
Thanks, anyway. Bye.

705
00:44:11,449 --> 00:44:12,781
Hello?

706
00:44:14,352 --> 00:44:15,615
Yeah?

707
00:44:17,021 --> 00:44:18,614
Oh, great.

708
00:44:18,789 --> 00:44:21,953
Great. Yes. Campbell,
I'll be right there.

709
00:44:22,126 --> 00:44:23,253
Okay.

710
00:44:23,427 --> 00:44:24,827
Bye.

711
00:44:46,851 --> 00:44:48,342
The phone work?

712
00:44:57,995 --> 00:44:59,725
Warden, the phone work?

713
00:45:00,598 --> 00:45:02,658
Yeah. Your daughter just called.

714
00:45:02,833 --> 00:45:05,803
Said police had an
address for Hock somebody.

715
00:45:05,970 --> 00:45:07,734
She's on her way there now.

716
00:45:18,182 --> 00:45:20,549
- Do they have him yet?
- Not yet. Officers just went in.

717
00:45:20,718 --> 00:45:22,718
- They know they gotta take him alive?
- They know.

718
00:45:22,887 --> 00:45:26,016
- He's on the fire escape!
- Campbell, no guns.

719
00:45:36,067 --> 00:45:37,865
He's coming down!

720
00:45:46,410 --> 00:45:47,410
Stay down!

721
00:45:50,881 --> 00:45:52,076
He's coming back!

722
00:45:59,890 --> 00:46:01,620
No!

723
00:46:08,766 --> 00:46:10,359
He's dead.

724
00:46:24,682 --> 00:46:27,345
9 mm Makarov.

725
00:46:34,659 --> 00:46:36,423
It's time.

726
00:46:50,975 --> 00:46:52,341
Yeah?

727
00:46:53,811 --> 00:46:55,245
Yes, sir.

728
00:46:59,617 --> 00:47:01,108
Yes, sir.

729
00:47:05,122 --> 00:47:07,455
They found the murder weapon.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>
